<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oswestry</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oswestry is a market town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, close to the Welsh border. Its name is linked with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald of Northumbria</a>, killed nearby at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maserfield">Battle of Maserfield</a> in 642. The town was held at times by English and Welsh rulers: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madog-ap-maredudd">Madog ap Maredudd</a> of Powys built a castle there around 1149, and it later became an English marcher lordship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oswestry.jpg" alt="Oswestry" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Broster · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Llwyd_Mansion_1604_Oswestry_(8689969578).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Oswestry (Welsh: Croesoswallt) is an ancient market town and historic railway town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, about five miles from the Welsh border, at the junction of the A5, A483 and A495 roads. It has a mixed English and Welsh heritage and is the largest settlement within the Oswestry Uplands. The name is first attested in 1191 as Oswaldestroe, from the Old English personal name Ōswald and trēow, meaning tree. The traditional Welsh name, first attested in 1254, means Oswald's cross, and since trēow may also mean cross, the name may have meant Oswald's cross in both languages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest known settlement is Old Oswestry, one of the best-preserved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hill forts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, with evidence of construction and occupation between 800 BCE and 43 CE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maserfield">Battle of Maserfield</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda of Mercia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald of Northumbria</a>, is widely thought to have been fought at Oswestry, although scholars debate its location. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> of 1086 records a castle built by Rainald, a Norman sheriff of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>. Henry I later granted the feudal barony of Oswestry to Alan fitz Flaad, a Breton knight.</p><p>The town changed hands between the English and the Welsh a number of times in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and still keeps some Welsh-language street and place names. In 1190 it was granted the right to hold a market each Wednesday. It built walls for protection, but Parliamentarians took the town from the Royalists after a brief siege on 22 June 1644 and tore the walls down in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>, leaving only the Newgate Pillar visible. Oswestry School, founded in 1407 by David Holbache, is the second oldest free school in the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Oswestry had a population of 17,509 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2021. The railway station, opened in 1840 on the main line of the Cambrian Railways, closed in 1966. St Oswald's Church, first mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a>, is Grade II* listed, with a tower from the late 12th or early 13th century. Old Oswestry dominates the northern and eastern approaches to the town. The poet Wilfred Owen (1893–1918) and the novelist Barbara Pym were born in Oswestry, and Park Hall, east of the town, served as an army training camp and military hospital from 1915.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oswestry” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later rulers, facing the Norman lords of the March, built castles of their own: Madog ap Maredudd raised a castle at <b>Oswestry</b> in about 1149.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Penda of Mercia, allied with the Welsh kingdom of Gwynedd, defeated King Oswald of Northumbria, probably at <b>Oswestry</b> in Shropshire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-5">5 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
